Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

register(DangleE);
define
    deFlag; deN;
    deArm code={
        deN = 5;
        if deFlag;
            deN = 1;
            deN = deN + 1;
        else            deN = 9;
        };
    deRun code={
        deFlag = 1;
        deArm();
        cerr "DE-1 unbraced arm, flag TRUE   deN = " deN " -- wants 2 (then-arm ran)":;
        deFlag = 0;
        deArm();
        cerr "DE-2 unbraced arm, flag FALSE  deN = " deN " -- wants 9. A 5 means the ELSE was DROPPED":;
        };
    ;
deRun();
print "DANGLINGE SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

danglingE -- KANT PARSES AN UNBRACED MULTI-STATEMENT if-ARM FOLLOWED BY AN else,
AND THE else FIRES. Promoted into the fleet 2026-09-15 from incant/fixits/danglingElse,
which retires to incant/attic on this row.

WHAT IT PINS, and why it is two rows rather than one. Bear-trap 32 says this exact
shape breaks the parse. On the TOK road it does -- measured 2026-09-10, canary 0 against
a braced control's 319. On the KANT road it does not, and the trap's discriminator is
backwards there: the BRACE is the trigger, not the cure. That inversion is recorded in
the trap itself; this file is the standing measurement underneath it.

⚠ DE-1 ALONE WOULD BE WORTHLESS. A parse that swallowed the else would print no error
and leave a plausible number -- deN would simply keep its initialiser. So both directions
are driven from one arm: DE-1 takes the then, DE-2 takes the else, and a 5 in DE-2 is the
initialiser showing through, which is what a dropped else looks like. The pair is the
assertion; neither row alone can see the defect.

⚠ WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT SAY. It does not say bear-trap 32 was wrong when written. It
was measured 2026-08-21 with two passing controls. Something between then and now changed
which shape kant refuses and NOTHING HERE IDENTIFIES WHAT -- the cause has not been looked
for, and the three places to look are named in the retired citizen's NEXT block.

⚠ A SPELLING TRAP PAID FOR TWICE. The first cut wrote `if deFlag` with NO SEMICOLON after
the condition, and every arm reported `ERROR processCode: parse failed` INCLUDING the
single-statement baseline -- which reads exactly like "the whole construct is broken" and
is really "the fixture is misspelt". Kant's if is `if <cond>; <statement>`. The tell is
that the arm you believe is the CONTROL fails too; when that happens, doubt the fixture
before the language.
